@flexiarg or3/design-around-the-red-line
@title Design Around the Partner's Red Line
@audience evaluation leads, trialists, community-partnered researchers
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🚧/线 🔀/变]
@see or3/co-design-is-not-consultation

! conclusion: Design Around the Partner's Red Line

  + context: Your preferred evaluation design asks a delivery partner to do something they believe is wrong.

  + IF:
    You have planned a controlled evaluation, and the design requires withholding the intervention from some participants — or otherwise asks the partner to act against the value that makes them a good partner in the first place.

  + HOWEVER:
    The partner declines, and the obvious readings are both bad: abandon the evaluation, or spend the relationship on persuading them. A charity that exists to widen access will not run a control arm that narrows it, and no amount of methodological explanation changes that.

  + THEN:
    Treat the refusal as a design constraint rather than an obstacle, and look for the comparison the world is already giving you. A staged roll-out is a natural experiment: sites not yet reached are a comparison group nobody was denied. Recruit additional comparison data from places the programme has not arrived. Write the constraint and the redesign into the methods section, so the adaptation is visible as reasoning rather than looking like a compromise.

  + BECAUSE:
    A partner's red line is information about how the intervention will actually be deployed, so a design that accommodates it usually has better external validity than the one you started with — and the relationship survives to support the next study.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Before finalising an evaluation design, ask each delivery partner which parts they would refuse, and design from there.]
    next[For an example of an RCT redesigned as a stepped-wedge study after a national charity declined a control arm, contact the First Steps team.]
